{"call_id": "call_20250806_204431_776171", "timestamp": "2025-08-06T20:44:31.776195", "agent_name": "planner", "status": "started", "input": "# Neural Architecture Evolution Mission\n\n## EXPERIMENTAL CONTEXT & HISTORICAL EVIDENCE\n## EXPERIMENTAL EVIDENCE PORTFOLIO\n\n### Experiment: hybrid_linear_hrm\n**Architecture Identifier**: hybrid_linear_hrm\n\n#### Performance Metrics Summary\n**Training Progression**: step,100,200,300,400,500\nloss,3.2,2.8,2.4,2.1,1.9\n**Evaluation Results**: Model,Reasoning,Efficiency\nhybrid_linear_hrm,0.78,0.95\n\n#### Implementation Analysis\n**Architecture Overview**: 309 lines of code implementing hybrid_linear_hrm\n**Code Preview** (use read_code_file for full implementation):\n```\n\"\"\"\nHybrid Linear-HRM Architecture Example\nDemonstrates integration of linear attention with hierarchical reasoning modules\nThis serves as a base architecture for ASI-Arch to evolve from.\n\"\"\"\nimport t...\n```\n\n**IMPORTANT**: This is only a preview. Use read_code_file() tool to get the complete current architecture.\n\n#### Synthesized Experimental Insights\nLinear-HRM Fusion Research Experience Synthesis\n\n## Performance Pattern Analysis\n\n### Strengths Identified:\n1. **Computational Efficiency**: Successfully maintained O(n) linear complexity through integration of linear attention with hierarchical reasoning modules\n2. **Reasoning Improvement**: Achieved higher accuracy on reasoning tasks compared to pure attention models, validating the hybrid approach\n3. **Multi-timescale Processing**: Effective implementation of System 1/System 2 dual-path architecture with L-module operating at every step and H-module at every 4 steps\n4. **Cross-modal Fusion**: Successful combination of attention and reasoning outputs through integration layer\n5. **State Persistence**: Effective hierarchical state management for maintaining reasoning context across time\n6. **Scalability**: Maintained linear computational complexity while scaling to larger inputs\n\n### Weaknesses Identified:\n1. **Convergence Detection Inefficiency**: The adaptive reasoning depth mechanism shows delayed convergence in some tasks\n2. **Working Memory Limitations**: Hierarchical state preservation isn't fully capturing long-range dependencies for memory-intensive tasks\n3. **System 2 Bottlenecks**: H-Module's strategic reasoning appears to be underutilized in certain scenarios, suggesting suboptimal cross-modal fusion\n4. **Task-Specific Performance Variation**: While reasoning improves overall, there are inconsistent gains across different task types (particularly in commonsense reasoning)\n5. **Resource Allocation Imbalance**: Some experiments show L-module dominating computation without adequate H-module engagement\n\n## Theoretical Validation Assessment\n\n### Alignment with Design Motivations:\n- The hybrid architecture successfully combines O(n) linear attention with hierarchical reasoning modules as intended\n- Multi-timescale processing was effectively implemented through the dual-path System 1/System 2 architecture\n- Cross-modal fusion demonstrated the expected synergies between attention and reasoning\n- Adaptive processing depths were achieved, though with room for improvement in convergence efficiency\n\n### Theoretical Expectations Confirmed:\n1. Linear attention maintains computational scalability across all tasks\n2. Hierarchical reasoning improves abstract pattern recognition capabilities\n3. Multi-timescale coordination enables both fast tactical and slow strategic processing\n4. Cross-modal fusion enhances the integration of different cognitive processes\n\n### Areas Where Hypotheses Were Refuted or Require Refinement:\n1. The convergence detection mechanism doesn't always adaptively engage H-Module optimally\n2. Working memory through hierarchical state preservation isn't fully capturing long-range dependencies\n3. Resource allocation between L-module and H-module sometimes becomes unbalanced\n4. Task-specific performance gains aren't as uniform as expected across different cognitive domains\n\n## Root Cause Analysis\n\n### Fundamental Architectural Limitations:\n1. **Convergence Detection**: The mechanism for determining when to engage deeper reasoning (H-Module) is too conservative, leading to underutilization in some scenarios\n2. **Working Memory Design**: The current hierarchical state management doesn't adequately maintain information across very long sequences\n3. **Cross-Modal Fusion Inefficiency**: Integration of attention and reasoning outputs could be more sophisticated to better leverage H-Module capabilities\n4. **Resource Allocation**: Lack of dynamic adjustment between L-module and H-module computational resources leads to suboptimal processing\n\n### Computational Bottlenecks:\n1. Excessive L-module computations when H-module should be more engaged\n2. Inefficient state updates in the hierarchical memory system for long-range dependencies\n3. Suboptimal cross-modal fusion operations that don't fully combine attention and reasoning features\n\n## Research Integration Opportunities\n\n### Mapping Weaknesses to Available Insights:\n1. **Convergence Detection Improvement**: Incorporate reinforcement learning approaches from cognitive control research to make engagement decisions more adaptive\n2. **Enhanced Working Memory**: Integrate neural cache mechanisms or transform-based memory networks that have shown success in long-range dependency modeling\n3. **Advanced Cross-Modal Fusion**: Adopt attention-based feature gating or adaptive weighting schemes from multi-modal processing research\n4. **Dynamic Resource Allocation**: Implement differentiable resource allocation strategies inspired by neural architecture search techniques\n5. **Task-Specific Adaptation**: Apply meta-learning approaches to calibrate the system for different task domains more effectively\n\n### Promising Research Directions:\n1. Cognitive control mechanisms that adaptively engage different processing modes\n2. Advanced memory architectures that better capture long-range dependencies\n3. Sophisticated cross-modal fusion techniques from multi-modal AI research\n4. Differentiable optimization strategies for resource allocation between processing modules\n5. Meta-learning approaches for task-specific calibration of cognitive architectures\n\n## Innovation Opportunity Identification\n\n### Concrete Architectural Improvements:\n1. **Enhanced Convergence Detection**: Develop a more adaptive mechanism using RL or meta-learning to better determine when deeper reasoning is needed\n2. **Advanced Working Memory System**: Implement neural cache mechanisms or transform-based memory networks for better long-range dependency modeling\n3. **Sophisticated Cross-Modal Fusion**: Design attention-based gating or adaptive weighting schemes for more effective integration of attention and reasoning\n4. **Dynamic Resource Allocation**: Create a differentiable system that can optimally balance computation between L-module and H-module based on task demands\n5. **Task-Specific Calibration**: Incorporate meta-learning components to automatically adjust architectural parameters for different task domains\n\n### Preservation Guidance:\n- Maintain the core O(n) linear attention mechanism as it provides critical scalability advantages\n- Preserve the dual-path System 1/System 2 architecture which enables effective multi-timescale processing\n- Keep the hierarchical state management system but enhance its capabilities with advanced memory techniques\n- Retain cross-modal fusion concept but improve its implementation through more sophisticated integration methods\n\n### Breakthrough Opportunities:\n1. Developing a unified learning algorithm that jointly optimizes both attention and reasoning components\n2. Creating adaptive convergence detection that can anticipate when deeper reasoning will be beneficial\n3. Designing a hybrid memory system that combines hierarchical state preservation with neural cache mechanisms\n4. Implementing a truly dynamic resource allocation system that adapts in real-time to task demands\n5. Establishing meta-learning protocols that can automatically calibrate the architecture for new tasks or domains\n\n## Implementation Strategy\n\n### Short-Term Improvements:\n1. Refine convergence detection using simple RL techniques to make H-Module engagement more adaptive\n2. Enhance working memory by incorporating basic neural cache mechanisms alongside existing hierarchical state management\n3. Improve cross-modal fusion with attention-based gating that better combines L-module and H-module outputs\n4. Implement a preliminary dynamic resource allocation system using learnable parameters\n\n### Medium-Term Developments:\n1. Integrate more sophisticated meta-learning components for task-specific calibration\n2. Develop advanced memory architectures that combine multiple complementary techniques\n3. Create unified learning algorithms that optimize both attention and reasoning simultaneously\n4. Refine adaptive convergence detection with more complex RL policies or predictive modeling\n\n### Long-Term Innovations:\n1. Design a fully integrated hybrid architecture where attention and reasoning are co-evolved during training\n2. Develop real-time dynamic resource allocation systems that adapt to task demands without explicit supervision\n3. Create self-calibrating architectures that can automatically adjust processing strategies based on task analysis\n4. Establish unified cognitive models that seamlessly integrate multiple timescales of processing with advanced memory systems\n\n## Final Recommendations for Planner:\n1. **Immediate Focus Areas**: Improve convergence detection, enhance working memory, and refine cross-modal fusion\n2. **Key Preservation Elements**: Maintain O(n) attention, dual-path architecture, and hierarchical state management\n3. **Research Integration Priorities**: Incorporate RL-based control mechanisms, neural cache memories, and attention-gated fusion\n4. **Long-Term Vision**: Develop co-evolved attention-reasoning systems with adaptive resource allocation and self-calibrating capabilities\n5. **Implementation Roadmap**: Start with incremental improvements to convergence detection and memory, then progress to more sophisticated integration techniques and unified learning algorithms\n\nThis synthesis provides a comprehensive guide for architectural evolution while maintaining computational efficiency and building on demonstrated successes.\n\n---\n\n## ARCHITECTURE EVOLUTION OBJECTIVE\nYour mission is to create a breakthrough neural architecture that addresses critical performance limitations identified through experimental evidence while integrating cutting-edge research insights. Design and implement an innovative architecture that maintains computational efficiency while achieving superior cognitive capabilities.\n\n## SYSTEMATIC EVOLUTION METHODOLOGY\n\n### PHASE 1: Evidence-Based Analysis Framework\n\n#### 1.1 Architecture Forensics\n**Current State Assessment:**\n- Use `read_code_file` to examine existing architectural implementations\n- Map computational mechanisms, design patterns, and information flow\n- Identify core algorithmic approaches and their theoretical foundations\n- Document interface constraints and compatibility requirements\n\n#### 1.2 Performance Pattern Recognition  \n**Historical Evidence Analysis:**\n- **Training Dynamics Diagnosis**: Extract optimization challenges from loss curves and convergence patterns\n- **Task-Specific Performance Profiling**: Identify capability gaps across cognitive domains (reasoning, memory, comprehension)\n- **Bottleneck Identification**: Pinpoint architectural elements limiting performance vs. those enabling strengths\n- **Cross-Architecture Comparison**: Analyze performance patterns across different experimental variants\n\n#### 1.3 Research Integration Strategy\n**Theoretical Foundation Building:**\n- Map research insights to observed performance limitations\n- Identify specific theoretical principles addressing architectural weaknesses  \n- Synthesize multiple research findings for comprehensive enhancement opportunities\n- Validate theoretical applicability through experimental evidence correlation\n\n### PHASE 2: Innovation Design Framework\n\n#### 2.1 Targeted Performance Engineering\n**Gap-Specific Solutions:**\n- Design architectural modifications targeting the most critical performance bottlenecks\n- Create mechanisms leveraging research insights for problematic capability domains\n- Balance multiple improvement objectives while maintaining architectural coherence\n- Ensure modifications address root causes rather than symptoms\n\n#### 2.2 Theoretical Grounding Protocol\n**Research-Driven Design:**\n- Ground all modifications in validated theoretical principles\n- Ensure mathematical and computational justification for proposed changes\n- Verify alignment with established research findings and best practices\n- Create novel combinations of insights for breakthrough potential\n\n#### 2.3 Efficiency Optimization Standards\n**Computational Constraints:**\n- Design using chunked computation patterns for scalability\n- Maintain sub-quadratic O(N log N) complexity throughout\n- Optimize memory usage through efficient processing strategies\n- Preserve performance gains within strict complexity bounds\n\n### PHASE 3: Implementation Excellence Protocol\n\n#### 3.1 Architecture Implementation Standards\n**Code Development Requirements:**\n- Use `write_code_file` to implement the complete evolved architecture\n- Preserve interface compatibility (forward function signatures, __init__ **kwargs)\n- Add new parameters with sensible defaults (enabled by default for new features)\n- Remove or refactor existing features to prevent architectural bloat\n- Implement proper causal masking and information flow constraints\n\n#### 3.2 Quality Assurance Framework\n**Technical Excellence Standards:**\n- Maintain @torch.compile decorators for computational optimization\n- Preserve chunked processing patterns throughout the architecture\n- Ensure causal constraints prevent any information leakage\n- Verify sub-quadratic complexity in all implemented operations\n\n#### 3.3 Documentation and Justification\n**Innovation Communication:**\n- Create comprehensive motivation explaining evolution rationale\n- Connect experimental evidence to theoretical insights and implementation decisions\n- Justify expected improvements based on research findings\n- Provide clear reasoning for all architectural design choices\n\n## TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION SPECIFICATIONS\n\n### Critical Preservation Requirements\n- **Class Structure**: Maintain DeltaNet class name and inheritance hierarchy\n- **Interface Stability**: Preserve exact forward function signature compatibility\n- **Parameter Compatibility**: Support **kwargs in __init__ for extensibility\n- **Compilation Strategy**: Apply @torch.compile selectively to core computational functions only\n- **Dimensional Consistency**: Maintain d_model and core parameter structure\n\n### Implementation Quality Standards\n- **Chunked Processing**: All sequence operations must utilize fixed-size chunking\n- **Causal Integrity**: Implement strict causal constraints in attention-like mechanisms\n- **Complexity Bounds**: Ensure O(N log N) or better for all operations\n- **Memory Efficiency**: Design for optimal memory usage with chunked patterns\n- **Compilation Safety**: Avoid @torch.compile on utility functions to prevent conflicts\n\n### MANDATORY: Tensor Operations Robustness\n- **einops.rearrange() Requirement**: Replace ALL .view()/.reshape() with einops.rearrange()\n- **Dynamic Dimension Handling**: Never manually calculate dimensions - use einops inference\n- **Batch Size Agnostic**: All operations must work with ANY batch size\n- **Runtime Shape Extraction**: Get dimensions from tensor.shape at runtime, not config\n- **Adaptive Processing